I am very new to S-Plus, and this seems like a very basic question, but I
have not found anything that addresses it in any of the manuals,
tutorials, and what-not that I have consulted.

I am using S-PLUS 6.2 on Windows. I have a data frame, x, imported from a
file which used numeric codes to indicate missing values.  The missing
value code is not the same for all columns - e.g. for some columns 0
indicates missing, for others it might be 9.

1. I cannot find anything in the GUI that would assign certain values to
NA.  Can this be done via the GUI?

2. After many hours of struggling I finally came up with the following
code to change the numeric codes to NA (Note that the columns which have
non-zero missing value codes are factors; the original 9's have become 3's
or 4's. Further, the process converts the factors back to numbers, so in
retrospect it clearly should have left everything numeric until after the
missing values were taken care of):

tmp.mv=matrix(c(0,0,3,3,3,0,0,4),nrow=nrow(x), ncol=ncol(x), byrow=T)
tmp.m=data.frame(1/(sapply(x[,1:8], FUN=as.numeric)!=tmp.mv))
x=data.frame(sapply(x[,1:8], FUN=as.numeric)*tmp.m)

Although this works, it seems so cumbersome and non-intuitive, that I have
to wonder whether there is a better way?
             - if I had lots of columns instead of just 8, with more
variety
of missing value codes, this would be highly error-prone
              - it creates two additional matrices/data frames the same
size
as the original, which seems rather inefficient, especially if the
original data frame is large.  (I have been avoiding loops because I am
told they are a "no-no" in S-Plus,  unless quite unavoidable.  Is it
really better to create two possibly large matrices just to avoid a loop?)

3.  After all that I tried to recreate the factor levels, and tried the
following line for 2 columns that have the same coding:

x[,3:4]=sapply(x[,3:4], FUN=factor, levels=c(1,2), labels=c("Yes","No"))

This made the factors "1" & "2" rather than "Yes" and "No".  But using
lapply gives the Yes/No labels.  Why is that?

Thanks for any advice/suggestions, etc.

