One other alternative is to use ImageMagick to convert EPS to WMF. I 
haven't tried it myself but the website claims the capability:

http://www.imagemagick.org/

Also note from ?postscript:

The postscript driver uses the header for Encapsulated PostScript when 
onefile=FALSE , and the other header when onefile=TRUE.

Thus you are already creating a eps file. However, I think in Word the 
default install does not include the drivers to read eps (at least on my 
machine) so you may have to reinstall Word to insert eps images. But 
doing so is tricky since if you pass the document to somebody else who 
does not have the driver they may be out of luck (I think).

