What Sundar describes is similar to my experience.  With the driver 
installed, Word will import the EPS graphic but the visual quality is 
really poor.  I suspect what is happening is that it converts it from the 
vector-graphics of PostScript (lines defined by beginning and endpoint, 
etc.) into a bitmap.  This implies a loss of quality as it gets further 
translated into different resolutions for printing or screen display.

More evidence: I notice a header line in a Word file containing an 
incorporated graphic (well, one for each graphic) as follows:

CREATOR: XV Version 3.10a  Rev: 12/29/94  Quality = 75, Smoothing = 0

I don't know whether this is the same xv that many of us have used to 
view and convert among graphic formats on Unix systems but it sure looks 
like a description of a conversion to a bitmapped format.  (And not a 
particularly contemporary one, either!)

I have resorted to outputting the Word file as PDF and then incorporating 
the PDF graphics using Adobe Acrobat, but this is obviously a far from 
optimal solution.

On Thu, 4 Nov 2004, Sundar Dorai-Raj wrote:
> The postscript driver uses the header for Encapsulated PostScript when
> onefile=FALSE , and the other header when onefile=TRUE.
>
> Thus you are already creating a eps file. However, I think in Word the
