Subject: Re: More GOF questions...
In-Reply-To: <6.1.2.0.2.20041104145145.02ca19f0@129.15.69.4>
References: <6.1.2.0.2.20041104145145.02ca19f0@129.15.69.4>
Message-ID: <007a01c4c35d$d46acb40$8f5a3f92@labor.ak>

You've probably gotten a dozen answers to this already, but I haven't
noticed any on the list, so here goes:

The KS test is not intended for distributions with mass points (i.e.,
discrete or mixed distributions).  See this nifty reference:
http://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda35g.htm .

You _can_ use the KS test with mixed or discrete distributions, _if_ you do
a Monte Carlo to give the critical values.  If you do that, it seems to work
considerably better than the Chi-Squared test for many cases.

Hope this helps,
Nels

