Kim:  If you are going to go the Monte Carlo resampling approach you will
be better off going to compiled Fortran code than trying to implement it in
S-Plus.  For these kind of interative, looping procedures, Fortran is much
faster than R which is much faster than S-Plus in my experience.  Paul
Mielke's web site (http://www.stat.colostate.edu/~mielke/permute.html) has
Fortran 77 code for Monte Carlo P-value approximation of Kolmogorov
goodness-of-fit test and others (see Mielke and Berry 2001.  Pemutation
methods:  A distance function approach.  Springer for details and power
comparisons).  However, I am not convinced that the permutation
approximation of P-values eliminates the fundamental problem of using these
continuous goodness-of-fit statistics with very discrete data (many tied
values).   Another possibiltiy occurred to me after I first responded to
you.  Perhaps you might dither the raw values by adding a small random
number to each, e.g., if you had integer counts (1, 2, ..., 9, 10) you
could add a uniform random number between -0.5 and 0.5 to each interger.
Not sure what perils might lurk in this approach.

Brian

