I guess you are trying to merge on something other than a `primary key',
that is with duplicates in one or both of your tables.  In that case you
will get a row in the result for each combination of rows in the two
tables that match the key.

A <- data.frame(a=c(1,1,2,3), b=letters[1:4])
B <- data.frame(a=1:2, c=c(T,F))
merge(A, B, by="a", all.x=FALSE, all.y=FALSE)

gives 3 rows, even though B has only two.

